  "question": {
    "text": "At time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>&nbsp;a particle starts travelling from a height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> in a plane keeping <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>z</mi></math> coordinate constant. At any instant of time, it&#39;s position along the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi></math> directions are defined as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>t</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math> respectively. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math> acceleration of the particle will be",
    "image": null
